server, ntdll: the wineserver keeps a thread table for FEX's helpers.

In b159 (FEX 159, Proton d2r9) FEX's own helper threads asked the wineserver about every
thread of D2R's process, round after round: the priority guard and thread placer walk the
threads with NtGetNextThread (get_next_thread 66/s) and NtQueryInformationThread
(ThreadBasicInformation, ThreadNameInformation: get_thread_info ~160/s), the stats log reads
ThreadTimes for every thread every second (get_thread_times) and closes the handles again
(close_handle). About 300 of the ~2,100 requests a second in play came from these helpers.

The wineserver owns all of these values, so it now writes them itself into a table that the
process maps read-only, at the moment one of them changes:
  thread created (create_thread), started (init_first_thread, init_thread, init_process_done:
  Linux tid and TEB), priority (set_thread_priority, so also base priority, priority class and
  process base priority changes), affinity (set_thread_affinity, so also process affinity
  changes), priority boost (set_thread_disable_boost, also through the process), name and
  debugger hiding (set_thread_info), killed (kill_thread: entry freed, and the next thread
  rewritten because it may be the process's first thread now, whose Priority has +1),
  destroyed (destroy_thread, a last check).
Each entry holds what get_thread_info and get_thread_times return for the thread at that
moment, so FEX gets exactly what NtQueryInformationThread would give it, FEX's own threads
included: thread id, Linux tid, TEB, Priority (get_effective_thread_priority), BasePriority,
the server's affinity (ThreadBasicInformation.AffinityMask = this & the system affinity mask,
which ntdll exports next to the table), creation time and the name. Nothing else changes for
the server or for the process; no request goes away by itself, FEX has to read the table.

The table (64 KB memfd, made on the process's first d2r_get_thread_table request, sealed
against shrinking and growing and against new writable mappings; the server keeps its own
copy of everything it needs and never reads the shared memory, so nothing the process does
to it can change what the server does):
  header, 128 bytes: version 1, header_size 128, entry_size 128, max_entries 511, used
  (entries [0, used) can be in use), count (threads listed now), changes (+1 after every
  entry write), not_listed (threads left out because the table is full; one gets the next
  free entry), pid, serial (serials handed out so far), reserved.
  entry, 128 bytes at 128 + 128 * i: seq (u32, odd while being written, +2 per write), tid
  (u32, 0 = free), unix_tid (s32, -1 until the thread runs), priority (s32), base_priority
  (s32), flags (u32: 1 first thread of the process, 2 priority boost disabled, 4 hidden from
  the debugger, 8 name cut or a character replaced), teb (u64), affinity (u64), serial (u32,
  the thread's number in the table, never reused, so a reused tid shows as a new thread),
  name_len (u32, whole name in characters), creation_time (s64), name (72 bytes, ASCII,
  '?' for other characters, NUL terminated, cut to 71).
Reading an entry: s0 = seq (acquire load); if odd, try again; copy the entry; acquire fence;
s1 = seq; keep the copy only when s0 == s1. A free entry (tid 0) means the thread is gone.

ntdll asks once at start (load_ntdll_functions, under fd_cache_mutex as other fd receives
do), maps the table read-only and writes into the new export __wine_d2r_threads (8 x ULONG64,
PE ntdll): version (u32, 1 once mapped, written last), size (u32), table (u64 address),
system_affinity (u64, get_system_affinity_mask()), status (u32, why there is no table when
version stays 0: NTSTATUS, or 0xe0000000 | errno of the mmap), pad, reserved[4].

The new request is appended after the last one, so every other request keeps its number;
SERVER_PROTOCOL_VERSION goes up by one (wineserver and ntdll are built together).

Model test: the server block, compiled unchanged from thread.c, runs in a wineserver model
whose thread and process functions copy thread.c and process.c with the hooks in place:
three processes made and ended over and over, up to 700 threads (more than the table
holds), tid reuse, creates, starts, kills, destroys, priority, base priority, priority
class, boost, thread and process affinity (also failing sched_setaffinity), names (empty,
71 and 90 characters, non-ASCII), debugger hiding, changes to killed threads through a
kept handle, the table asked for at any point of a process's start or later. After every
step every table is read through a read-only mapping and compared with what get_thread_info
and get_thread_times would return for each live thread: 200 programs of 3,000 steps, 0
differences; with any one hook taken out (affinity, boost, name, first thread after a kill)
37 to 38 of 40 programs fail, so the test sees each of them. A writable mapping and a shrink
of the client's fd are refused. Seqlock test: the server code rewrites entries as fast as it
can (31 million writes in 8 s) while three reader threads use the read rule above: 0 torn
copies kept; with the odd mark taken out, 10,438.

WINE_D2R_THREAD_TABLE=0: ntdll does not ask for the table (the export stays version 0) and
the server never makes one. Read once per process.
